# Working with dude

This project pins its own toolchain: the `dude` CLI and the `tauri` stack are
devDependencies in `package.json`, mirrored in `dude.json`. Everyone who runs
`pnpm install` gets the exact same versions.

## Installation

Install the launcher once, globally:

```bash
export GITHUB_TOKEN=<personal access token with read:packages>
npm install -g @cubocicloide/dude-launcher
```

The launcher finds the nearest `dude.json`, provisions the pinned toolchain if
needed, and re-execs the project-local `dude`. Different projects can pin
different versions — no switching required.

## Everyday commands

```bash
dude dev       # run the desktop app (hot-reload)
dude build     # build installers
dude doctor    # check the toolchain
dude lint      # structural conventions
dude format    # prettier + cargo fmt
dude review    # everything, one pass
dude test      # cargo test
dude docs      # this site, live-reload
dude help      # live command catalog
```

## Upgrading the toolchain

```bash
dude upgrade                 # bump CLI + stack pins interactively
dude upgrade --stack         # stack only
pnpm install                 # apply the new pins
```

`dude upgrade` updates `package.json` and `dude.json` in lockstep. It does not
migrate scaffolded files; roll back by pinning the previous version again.

## Custom commands

Any TypeScript file in `.dude/commands/` becomes a project-local `dude`
command (loaded with jiti — real TS, project imports allowed). See
`.dude/commands/README.md` for the contract, and `hello.ts` for a working
example. Custom commands override stack commands of the same name.

## Project lint rules

`dude lint` also runs any rules you define under `.dude/lint/checks/` — the
rule code is derived from the path (`.dude/lint/checks/PRJ/001.ts` → `PRJ001`)
and the contract is the same one the stack's own checks use. See
`.dude/lint/checks/README.md` and the `PRJ/001.ts` example. To disable a
built-in rule, list its code in `dude.json`:

```json
{ "lint": { "disable": ["FE003"] } }
```
